# errors-ownership.topic -- the diagnostics of affine values, coroutines,
# mutable places, traits and context traits, one topic per code.

@topic error.use-after-move
title: USE-AFTER-MOVE: an affine value is used after it was moved
summary: A binding whose affine value (a coroutine handle, or a struct, List or vector owning one) was moved to another owner is used again.
index: errors
alias: used after it was moved; moved coroutine used again; use of a moved binding
keywords: affine, move, moved, owner, coroutine, handle, argument, destructuring, dead binding
related: rule.affine-use concept.affine proof.ownership concept.coroutines error.affine-not-definitely-live error.affine-field-move-requires-destructure
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC USE-AFTER-MOVE

== MEANING
An affine value has exactly one usable owner. Binding it to another name
(`other = step`), passing it as an argument, storing it in a struct field,
a List literal or a MutableVector, returning it, or destructuring the
struct that holds it MOVES it: the new owner owns it, and the old binding
is dead from then on. A later use of the old binding -- a resume,
`coroutine::done?`, a field read, another move -- is USE-AFTER-MOVE. The
message prints where the value was moved and where it was used.

The rule exists because a coroutine handle is the state of one running
execution: two usable names for it would let two parts of the program
resume or release one continuation. Ownership is static only: nothing at
run time records a move, so every use of a dead owner is rejected at
compile time.

Not moves: resuming a handle (`step()`), `coroutine::done?(step)`,
reading an unrestricted field (`job.name`) and `list::length(xs)` keep
the owner owning. An affine value may be dropped unused: it is released
where its owner dies.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a handle passed to a function and then resumed in the caller: the
    callee's parameter owns it now;
  * `other = step` followed by `step()` (resume `other`);
  * a handle stored in a struct or a List literal, and the old name used;
  * a struct used after it was destructured: `{step} = job` consumes
    `job`, so even `job.name` afterwards is a use after move.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message prints the move ("moved here", with the statement that moved
the value) and the use ("used here"). Decide which owner should hold the
value after the move.

== WHAT TO DO
Use the new owner's name. If a callee must hand the value back, give it
a result of the same affine type, return the parameter (`return s`), and
bind the call's result to a new name (`next = advance(step)`; a binding
is never reassigned). When the caller must keep the value, pass the
callee only what it needs (an unrestricted field, a resume's result).

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine {step} = counter()
other = step
step()
@caption `other = step` moves the handle: `step` is dead afterwards
@error CORE SEMANTIC USE-AFTER-MOVE
@at 9:1
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine {step} = counter()
other = step
other()
@caption The new owner resumes the same coroutine
@value 3
@end

@topic error.affine-not-definitely-live
title: AFFINE-NOT-DEFINITELY-LIVE: an affine value moved on some path only
summary: An affine binding is used where it may already have been moved: a move on one branch, in a handler or in an earlier loop iteration reaches the use.
index: errors
alias: moved on some path; maybe moved affine value; conditionally moved coroutine
keywords: affine, move, branch, if, handler, loop, join, owner, liveness
related: error.use-after-move rule.affine-use concept.affine proof.ownership error.affine-path-release-unsupported
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-NOT-DEFINITELY-LIVE

== MEANING
The ownership analysis follows each affine binding through the code in
evaluation order. Where paths join (after an `if`, a handled call or a
loop), a binding moved on some paths but not on others is "maybe moved".
Using it there is AFFINE-NOT-DEFINITELY-LIVE: on one of the paths it is
already dead. There is no run-time moved flag to test, so the compiler
cannot let the program find out at run time; it rejects the use and lists
every move that can reach it.

A path that does not complete normally -- one that ends in `return`,
`fail`, `break` or `continue` -- does not reach the join, so a move inside
a branch that then returns leaves the binding live on the other path.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * an `if` without `else` (or a branch of an if/else) that moves the
    value, followed by a use after the `if`;
  * a handler (`on E:`) that moves the value, followed by a use;
  * a move inside a loop body: the next iteration sees the binding moved
    by the previous one (the diagnostic then points at the move itself).

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Each "moved here" line is a move on some path that reaches the use. In
a loop the move and the use can be the same expression: an earlier
iteration moved the value.

== WHAT TO DO
  * end the moving branch with `return` (or `break`/`continue`/`fail`), so
    only the paths that still own the value reach the use;
  * move the value on every path (an if/else whose branches both pass it
    on), or on none;
  * move it once, after the loop, instead of inside the loop body.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn finish(s: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
    s()

fn run(early: bool) -> int:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    if early:
        finish(step)
    step()

run(true)
@caption The `if` moves `step` on one path only
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-NOT-DEFINITELY-LIVE
@at 14:5
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn finish(s: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
    s()

fn run(early: bool) -> int:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    if early:
        return finish(step) * 10
    step()

[run(true), run(false)]
@caption The moving branch returns: the other path still owns `step`
@value [30, 3]
@end

@topic error.affine-capture-unsupported
title: AFFINE-CAPTURE-UNSUPPORTED: a closure captures an affine value
summary: A nested function refers to an affine binding of an enclosing scope; a closure may run any number of times and be copied freely, so it cannot own an affine value.
index: errors
alias: closure captures a coroutine; capture of an affine binding
keywords: affine, closure, nested function, capture, coroutine, owner
related: concept.closures concept.affine rule.affine-use proof.ownership error.mutable-place-capture
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-CAPTURE-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
A nested function that refers to a binding of its enclosing function (or
a top-level function that refers to a top-level binding) captures it.
Capturing an affine value is not supported: a closure is an ordinary,
unrestricted function value that may be called any number of times and
copied freely, so a captured handle would get several owners. Closure
values stay unrestricted; there are no affine (call-once) closures.

The one capture that is allowed is internal: a coroutine construction
captures its own arguments, because it runs exactly once.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a helper declared inside a function that resumes or inspects a
    coroutine of the enclosing function;
  * a top-level function that uses a handle bound at the top level.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass the value to the nested function as an argument (its parameter then
owns it, and returns it if the caller needs it back), or construct the
coroutine inside the nested function.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn run() -> int:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    fn next() -> int:
        step()
    next()

run()
@caption `next` would capture the handle of its enclosing function
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-CAPTURE-UNSUPPORTED
@at 10:9
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn run() -> int:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    fn next(s: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
        s()
    next(step)

run()
@caption Passed as an argument instead, the handle moves into `next`
@value 3
@end

@topic error.affine-container-unsupported
title: AFFINE-CONTAINER-UNSUPPORTED: affine values in a set or recursive struct
summary: An ImmutableSet of affine elements, or an affine struct that holds its own type in a List, MutableVector or MutableArray, is not supported.
index: errors
alias: set of coroutines; recursive affine struct
keywords: affine, ImmutableSet, set, recursive struct, tree, container, release, descriptor
related: concept.affine concept.immutable-set concept.structs concept.mutable-vector error.affine-equality-unsupported
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-CONTAINER-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
Reported where the type is written, for two forms:

  * `ImmutableSet[T]` with an affine T, in any position (a parameter, a
    field, a result, an element type). A set compares and hashes its
    members, and an affine value has no equality or hash
    (error.affine-equality-unsupported), so a set cannot own one. A List,
    a MutableVector and a MutableArray can: they are affine exactly when
    their element type is.
  * an affine struct that holds values of its own type in a List,
    MutableVector or MutableArray, directly or through other structs (a
    tree of coroutines). This is a limit of the implementation: an affine
    value is released by a static, finite walk of its type, and such a
    value nests to any depth. An unrestricted struct may hold itself in a
    container, and an affine struct may name itself through plain fields.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a set of handles used to track "which workers are running";
  * a task tree whose nodes own coroutines and hold their children in a
    List[Node].

== WHAT TO DO
For a set: keep the affine values in a List or MutableVector and track
membership by an unrestricted key (an int or str stored beside each
value). For a recursive structure: keep the coroutines out of it -- store
them in one MutableVector and let the tree hold their indices.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn size(s: ImmutableSet[Coroutine{args: [], return: int}]) -> int:
    1

0
@caption A set would have to compare and hash its coroutine members
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-CONTAINER-UNSUPPORTED
@at 7:8
@end

@example reject
struct Node:
    step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}
    children: List[Node]

0
@caption An affine struct holding its own type in a List
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-CONTAINER-UNSUPPORTED
@at 3:15
@end

@topic error.affine-duplication-unsupported
title: AFFINE-DUPLICATION-UNSUPPORTED: one affine value in several slots
summary: mutable_array::create(n, value) places one value in every slot; an affine value would get several owners, so it must be made once per slot with mutable_array::generate.
index: errors
alias: mutable_array::create with a coroutine; affine value repeated in an array
keywords: affine, MutableArray, create, generate, factory, repeat, slots, coroutine
related: concept.mutable-array concept.affine stdlib.mutable_array::create stdlib.mutable_array::generate error.affine-erasure-unsupported
docs: MUTABLE-ARRAY.md AFFINE-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-DUPLICATION-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
An operation that logically duplicates its argument -- registered with
the `repeat` ownership role; today that is the value of
`mutable_array::create(n, value)` -- cannot take an affine value: every
slot would hold the same coroutine, one value with several owners. For an
unrestricted value the slots are logical copies and nothing is wrong. The
check follows the value through user functions: a wrapper that passes its
parameter on to `create` is rejected for an affine argument too.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * building a pool of workers with `mutable_array::create(n, make())`.

== WHAT TO DO
Make one value per slot with a factory: `mutable_array::generate(n, f)`
calls `f(i)` once for each slot, in index order, and moves each result
into its slot. The factory is an ordinary named function taking the
index; it cannot itself be affine.

@example reject
import coroutine
import mutable_array

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

slots = mutable_array::create(2, make())
0
@caption One coroutine would be placed in both slots
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-DUPLICATION-UNSUPPORTED
@at 12:34
@end

@example accept
import coroutine
import mutable_array

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make(i: int) -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

slots = mutable_array::generate(2, make)
results = loop step in slots:
    step()
results
@caption A factory makes one coroutine per slot; the loop consumes them
@value [3, 3]
@end

@topic error.affine-element-copy-out
title: AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT: reading an affine element would copy it out
summary: at, freeze or copy of a MutableVector or MutableArray of affine elements would make a second owner of an element the collection still owns; move it out instead.
index: errors
alias: mutable_vector::at of a coroutine; copy an affine element out of a vector
keywords: affine, MutableVector, MutableArray, at, freeze, copy, take, pop, swap, element
related: concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array concept.affine stdlib.mutable_vector::take stdlib.mutable_vector::pop stdlib.mutable_vector::swap stdlib.mutable_array::swap error.affine-list-operation-unsupported
docs: MUTABLE-VECTOR.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT

== MEANING
A MutableVector[T] or MutableArray[T] of an affine T owns its elements.
An operation that reads elements out while the collection keeps owning
them -- `at`, `mutable_array::freeze`, the source of `mutable_array::copy`
-- would give an element a second owner, so it is rejected for an affine
collection. There are no references to elements: an element leaves the
collection only by an operation that moves it out.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `queue.at(i)` to look at a coroutine in a scheduler queue;
  * freezing an array of coroutines into a List.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the element out:
  * MutableVector: `take(i)` removes element i, `pop()` the last one,
    `swap(i, replacement)` exchanges it for another value;
  * MutableArray: `swap(i, replacement)` (an array has no empty slot);
  * either: `loop x in v:` consumes the collection, moving each element
    into the loop variable in order.
Put the element back with `push` (vector) or `swap` when the collection
should keep it.

@example reject
import coroutine
import mutable_vector

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

fn first() -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    queue = mutable_vector::from_list([make(), make()])
    step = queue.at(0)
    step()

0
@caption `at` would copy the coroutine out while the vector keeps it
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT
@at 14:12
@end

@example accept
import coroutine
import mutable_vector

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

fn first() -> int errors IndexNotFound:
    queue = mutable_vector::from_list([make(), make()])
    step = queue.take(0)
    step() + queue.length()

first():
    on IndexNotFound:
        -1
@caption `take(0)` moves the element out; one is left in the vector
@value 4
@end

@topic error.affine-equality-unsupported
title: AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED: comparing or hashing an affine value
summary: ==, hash and the ImmutableSet operations are rejected for an affine value (a coroutine handle, or a struct or List owning one): it has no equality and no hash.
index: errors
alias: compare two coroutines; equality of coroutine handles
keywords: affine, equality, ==, !=, hash, ImmutableSet, coroutine, identity
related: rule.equality concept.affine concept.coroutines error.affine-container-unsupported stdlib.immutable_set::from_list
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
Comparing two affine values with `==` (or `!=`), hashing one, or giving
one to `immutable_set::from_list` or `immutable_set::contains` is
rejected: the result would observe the identity of the execution the
value owns, and a coroutine handle has no value equality. The check also
applies inside a generic function: a call that gives it an affine
argument it compares is rejected at the call.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * testing whether two variables hold "the same" coroutine;
  * comparing whole structs, one field of which is a handle.

== WHAT TO DO
Compare unrestricted data instead: give each worker an int or str
identity in a struct field beside its handle and compare that, or use
`coroutine::done?` to ask about a coroutine's state.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine {step: a} = counter()
coroutine {step: b} = counter()
a == b
@caption Two coroutine handles cannot be compared
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-EQUALITY-UNSUPPORTED
@at 9:1
@end

@topic error.affine-erasure-unsupported
title: AFFINE-ERASURE-UNSUPPORTED: an affine value where ownership is lost
summary: An affine value reaches a position whose type does not carry ownership -- any, an untyped native argument, a non-affine field, element or result type, the program's result, a module binding.
index: errors
alias: coroutine passed as any; affine value erased; coroutine as the program result
keywords: affine, erasure, any, untyped, program result, module binding, factory, declined instance, coroutine
related: concept.affine proof.ownership rule.affine-use concept.specialization error.affine-instance-budget error.affine-generic-nested-unsupported
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-ERASURE-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
Ownership is tracked through types: a binding, a parameter, a result, a
struct field or a List, MutableVector or MutableArray element of an
affine type owns the value. A position whose type is not affine (`any`,
or a type that cannot hold the value) does not carry ownership, so the
value would be duplicated or lost there. The message names the position:

  * a parameter declared `any` (or another non-affine type), an argument
    of a native that takes any value, an argument of a function value
    whose parameter types are unknown;
  * a struct field, a List element or a function result whose type is not
    affine (`[step, 2]` is a List of any; an `if` whose other branch is an
    int has type any);
  * the program's result: a result is shown, never owned;
  * a module binding: module values are immutable and shared by every
    user of the module;
  * an affine callable as the factory of `mutable_array::generate`,
    which calls it once per element;
  * an untyped parameter of a function whose specialization for this
    argument was declined ("its semantic instance was declined"). This is
    a limit of the implementation: a generic (untyped) function given an
    affine argument is specialized for it, and that analysis has a
    nesting limit -- a chain of about two dozen untyped functions handing
    the value on reaches it.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * ending the program (or a test) with a handle;
  * a helper `fn keep(x: any)` or a struct field typed `any`;
  * mixing a handle and other values in one List literal or `if`.

== WHAT TO DO
Give the position an affine type: declare the parameter, field or result
as `Coroutine{...}` (or the affine struct, List or vector type), or keep
the value in a binding. End the program with an unrestricted value (a
result of resuming, not the handle). For a declined specialization,
declare the parameter's type: a typed parameter needs no specialization.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn keep(x: any) -> int:
    1

coroutine {step} = counter()
keep(step)
@caption A parameter declared `any` cannot own the handle
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-ERASURE-UNSUPPORTED
@at 11:6
@end

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine {step} = counter()
step
@caption The program's result is shown, never owned
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-ERASURE-UNSUPPORTED
@at 8:1
@end

@topic error.affine-field-move-requires-destructure
title: AFFINE-FIELD-MOVE-REQUIRES-DESTRUCTURE: an affine field projected
summary: s.field of an affine field would make a second owner of what the struct owns; an affine field leaves a struct only through a destructuring that consumes it.
index: errors
alias: read a coroutine field of a struct; move a field out of a struct
keywords: affine, field, projection, struct, destructuring, partial move, coroutine
related: concept.destructuring concept.structs concept.affine error.use-after-move error.affine-temporary-unsupported
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-FIELD-MOVE-REQUIRES-DESTRUCTURE

== MEANING
A struct that has an affine field owns the field's value. Reading that
field with a projection (`job.step`) outside a destructuring would leave
two owners -- the new binding and the struct, which stays usable -- and
the ownership analysis has no "partially moved struct" state. The only
way to move an affine field out is a destructuring, which consumes the
whole struct: `{step} = job` moves `step` out and releases the other
affine fields at once. Reading an unrestricted field (`job.name`) is
fine and leaves the struct live.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `s = job.step` or `job.step()` on a struct holding a coroutine;
  * `coroutine::done?(job.step)`.

== WHAT TO DO
Destructure the struct, as the message suggests (`{step} = job`, or
`{step: s, name} = job` to rename and keep other fields). If the struct
is needed afterwards, build a new one from the parts:
`again = Job {name: name, step: s}`.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

struct Job:
    name: str
    step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}

coroutine {step} = counter()
job = Job {name: "count", step: step}
s = job.step
s()
@caption A projection cannot move the coroutine out of `job`
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-FIELD-MOVE-REQUIRES-DESTRUCTURE
@at 13:5
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

struct Job:
    name: str
    step: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}

coroutine {step} = counter()
job = Job {name: "count", step: step}
{step: s} = job
s()
@caption A destructuring consumes `job` and moves the field out
@value 3
@end

@topic error.affine-generic-nested-unsupported
title: AFFINE-GENERIC-NESTED-UNSUPPORTED: an affine argument to a nested generic
summary: An affine argument at an untyped parameter needs a per-type specialization, which a function declared inside another function (or one requiring a context trait) does not get.
index: errors
alias: nested generic function with a coroutine; untyped parameter of a nested function
keywords: affine, generic, untyped parameter, nested function, specialization, context trait, clone
related: concept.specialization concept.affine concept.functions error.affine-erasure-unsupported error.trait-nested-polymorphic
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-GENERIC-NESTED-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
A generic (untyped) function given an affine argument is specialized for
it: the compiler makes a clone of the function with the argument's type,
so the clone owns and releases it correctly. Two kinds of function are
not specialized per argument type, and giving them an affine value at an
untyped parameter is rejected:

  * a function declared inside another function: its specializations
    would have to be made per activation of the enclosing function;
  * a function whose context requirement includes a context trait: it is
    specialized once for its installed context, not per argument type.

This is a limit of the implementation, not a language rule.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a small untyped helper (`fn drive(c): c()`) declared inside the
    function that owns the coroutine.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the parameter's type (`c: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}`): a
typed parameter owns the argument with no specialization. Or move the
function to the top level of the module or program.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn run() -> int:
    fn drive(c):
        c()
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    drive(step)

run()
@caption An untyped parameter of a nested function cannot take the handle
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-GENERIC-NESTED-UNSUPPORTED
@at 11:5
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn run() -> int:
    fn drive(c: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
        c()
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    drive(step)

run()
@caption With a declared parameter type no specialization is needed
@value 3
@end

@topic error.affine-instance-budget
title: AFFINE-INSTANCE-BUDGET: no specialization for an affine argument
summary: A call gives an affine argument to an untyped parameter, but the compiler declined to specialize it; there is no run-time fallback, so the call is rejected.
index: errors
alias: affine specialization budget; declined specialization of an affine call
keywords: affine, generic, untyped parameter, specialization, budget, semantic instance, nesting limit
related: error.affine-erasure-unsupported error.trait-instance-budget concept.specialization concept.affine
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-INSTANCE-BUDGET

== MEANING
A call that gives an affine value to an untyped parameter needs a
specialization of the callee for the value's type (a clone that owns it);
there is no run-time ownership tracking and no run-time dispatch between
callable kinds to fall back on. The compiler makes these specializations
with its semantic-instance analysis, which has budgets (a nesting limit).
When the analysis declined such a call, the specialization plan reports
AFFINE-INSTANCE-BUDGET.

In practice a declined instance is reported earlier, by the ownership
check, as AFFINE-ERASURE-UNSUPPORTED ("its semantic instance was
declined"); this code is the plan's own check of the same condition, and
no small program is known to reach it. It is a limit of the compiler,
not a language rule.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * those of the declined-instance case of AFFINE-ERASURE-UNSUPPORTED,
    which reports them first: a long chain of untyped (generic)
    functions handing an affine value on, each specialized inside the
    previous one.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the type of the parameter that receives the affine value (a
typed parameter needs no specialization), or shorten the chain of untyped
functions the value passes through.

@topic error.affine-list-operation-unsupported
title: AFFINE-LIST-OPERATION-UNSUPPORTED: elements of an affine List accessed
summary: list::at, list::append, iterating a List of affine values and collecting affine values in a loop would copy elements a List still owns; a List of them only moves whole.
index: errors
alias: list::at of a coroutine; iterate a list of coroutines; collect coroutines in a loop
keywords: affine, List, list::at, list::append, loop, iteration, collecting loop, MutableVector, coroutine
related: concept.lists concept.mutable-vector concept.affine error.affine-element-copy-out stdlib.mutable_vector::from_list stdlib.list::at
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md MUTABLE-VECTOR.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-LIST-OPERATION-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
A List is immutable, and a List of affine values owns them: it moves
whole (bound, passed, returned) and releases every element where it
dies, and `list::length` reads it. Every operation that would copy an
element out while the List keeps owning it is rejected:

  * `list::at`, `list::append` and the other List functions;
  * `loop s in xs:` over a List of affine values (the loop variable would
    be a second owner);
  * a collecting loop whose body produces affine values (its List would
    own them across abrupt exits it cannot release yet).

Lists have no ownership-moving element operations; MutableVector does.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * keeping workers in a List literal and picking one with `list::at`;
  * `loop s in steps:` to resume each coroutine of a List;
  * `loop i from 0 to n: make()` to start n coroutines.

== WHAT TO DO
Use a MutableVector: `mutable_vector::from_list(xs)` moves the List into
a vector, whose `take`, `pop` and `swap` move elements out and whose
`loop s in v:` consumes it element by element. To collect values in a
loop, `push` each one into a vector.

@example reject
import coroutine
import list

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

steps = [make(), make()]
first = list::at(steps, 0)
0
@caption `list::at` would copy a coroutine the List still owns
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-LIST-OPERATION-UNSUPPORTED
@at 13:18
@end

@example accept
import coroutine
import mutable_vector

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

steps = [make(), make()]
queue = mutable_vector::from_list(steps)
loop step in queue:
    step()
@caption A vector made from the List is consumed one element at a time
@value [3, 3]
@end

@topic error.affine-path-release-unsupported
title: AFFINE-PATH-RELEASE-UNSUPPORTED: nowhere to release a value on one path
summary: A value moved on some paths through a statement must be released on the others, but a handled call's normal completion or a counting loop's end has no statement to hold the release.
index: errors
alias: moved in a handler but not on success; moved before break in a counting loop
keywords: affine, release, path, handler, break, loop, join, move, coroutine
related: error.affine-not-definitely-live concept.affine proof.ownership concept.loops concept.errors
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md MUTABLE-VECTOR.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-PATH-RELEASE-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
An affine value is released where its owner dies. When the last
statement that uses a binding moves it on some paths only, the compiler
releases it at the end of each path that still owns it: the last
statement of an `if` branch (an empty `else` gets one), the end of a
handler body, the `break` of an infinite `loop:`. Two kinds of path end
in no statement of their own, so there is nowhere to put the release:

  * a handled call that completes normally while a handler moved the
    value;
  * a counting or iterating loop (`loop i from ...`, `loop x in ...`)
    that runs to its end while a `break` path moved the value.

This is a limit of the implementation: the program is meaningful, but
the compiler cannot place the release yet.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * an `on E:` handler that hands the coroutine to a cleanup function;
  * a search loop that moves the value and breaks when it finds a match.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the value on every path, or on none. For a handler: bind the call's
result first and move the value in an `if` on that result. For a loop:
use an infinite `loop:` whose every exit is a `break`, or decide inside
the loop and move the value after it.

@example reject
import coroutine

error Boom

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn finish(s: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
    s()

fn risky(n: int) -> int errors Boom:
    if n > 0:
        fail Boom
    n

fn run(n: int) -> int:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    risky(n):
        on Boom:
            finish(step)

run(1)
@caption The handler moves `step`; the normal completion still owns it
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-PATH-RELEASE-UNSUPPORTED
@at 19:5
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

error Boom

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn finish(s: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
    s()

fn risky(n: int) -> int errors Boom:
    if n > 0:
        fail Boom
    n

fn run(n: int) -> int:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    status = risky(n):
        on Boom:
            -1
    if status < 0:
        finish(step)
    else:
        status

[run(1), run(0)]
@caption Bind the result, then move the value in an if: the else releases it
@value [3, 0]
@end

@topic error.affine-temporary-unsupported
title: AFFINE-TEMPORARY-UNSUPPORTED: a fresh affine value with no owner
summary: A fresh affine value (a call's result, a construction) is given to an operation that only observes it, or a field of it is read, so nothing would own it.
index: errors
alias: done? of a fresh coroutine; field of a fresh affine struct
keywords: affine, temporary, owner, coroutine::done?, field, observe, bind first
related: concept.affine rule.affine-use error.affine-field-move-requires-destructure stdlib.coroutine::done?
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-TEMPORARY-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
An operation that does not take ownership -- `coroutine::done?`, a
length, a read of a field -- needs an owner that keeps the value, so its
operand must be a binding that owns it. A fresh value, such as the
result of `make()`, has no owner there: the compiler would have no
binding to release it from afterwards, and the rest of a struct whose
field is read would be lost.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `coroutine::done?(make())`;
  * `make_job().name` on a function returning an affine struct.

== WHAT TO DO
Bind the value to a name first, then observe the binding:
`step = make()` and `coroutine::done?(step)`; `job = make_job()` and
`job.name`. The binding owns the value and releases it after its last
use.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

coroutine::done?(make())
@caption `done?` only observes; the fresh coroutine would have no owner
@error CORE SEMANTIC AFFINE-TEMPORARY-UNSUPPORTED
@at 11:18
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

step = make()
coroutine::done?(step)
@caption Bound to a name, the coroutine has an owner
@value false
@end

@topic error.mutable-place-receiver
title: MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER: a mutation's receiver is not a place
summary: A MutableVector or MutableArray operation that mutates must be applied to a place -- a local, parameter or context parameter of the same function, or a field path from one.
index: errors
alias: push to a temporary vector; mutate a captured vector; push as a function value
keywords: MutableVector, MutableArray, push, set, swap, take, pop, place, receiver, temporary, nested function, module binding, function value
related: concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array concept.values error.mutable-place-capture stdlib.mutable_vector::push
docs: MUTABLE-VECTOR.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER

== MEANING
MutableVector[T] and MutableArray[T] are mutable VALUES, not references:
a mutation (push, pop, take, swap, clear; set, swap, copy into) changes
the header of the place it is applied to, in place, and never stores a
new value anywhere else. So the receiver must be a place this function
owns: a local binding, a parameter or a context parameter of the same
function, or a chain of fields from one (`state.queue.push(x)`), whose
binding's static type is the collection type. The message says which
rule the receiver breaks:

  * a temporary value (a call's result, a List element): the update
    would be lost;
  * a binding of an enclosing function, from a nested function: a
    collection is a value; the nested function cannot mutate the copy
    its enclosing function sees;
  * a module binding, which every user of the module shares;
  * a binding whose static type is not the collection type (an `any`
    narrowed by a test): bind the narrowed value to a name first;
  * no receiver at all: the operation itself is used as a value (bound
    to a name, passed as an argument, stored in a field). An operation
    that mutates is only ever called, on a place: a call through a
    function value names no place, so it would update whatever
    collection its caller passes -- a copy, whose update is lost, or a
    place's own header behind the copies of it. The diagnostic is at the
    reference to the operation.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `make_vector().push(x)`;
  * a nested helper that pushes to its enclosing function's vector;
  * `p = mutable_vector::push`, or `apply(mutable_vector::push, v, x)`.

== WHAT TO DO
Bind the value to a name, mutate the name, and use the name afterwards.
For a helper, pass the collection in and return the updated one, or keep
it in a field of a context struct: a function mutates it through its
context parameter (`log.lines.push(n)`), and every function sharing the
installed context sees the change. To pass a mutation around, wrap it in a
function of your own that takes the collection and returns the updated
one; the operations that only read (`length`, `at`) may be values.

@example reject
import mutable_vector

fn fresh() -> MutableVector[int]:
    mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])

fn grow() -> int:
    fresh().push(3)
    0

grow()
@caption The pushed element would be lost with the temporary vector
@error CORE SEMANTIC MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER
@at 7:5
@end

@example accept
import mutable_vector

fn fresh() -> MutableVector[int]:
    mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])

fn grow() -> int:
    v = fresh()
    v.push(3)
    v.length()

grow()
@caption Bound to a name, the vector is a place
@value 3
@end

@example reject
import mutable_vector

fn grow() -> int:
    v = mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2])
    p = mutable_vector::push
    p(v, 3)
    v.length()

grow()
@caption Through a function value, push would have no place to update
@error CORE SEMANTIC MUTABLE-PLACE-RECEIVER
@at 5:9
@end

@topic error.mutable-place-capture
title: MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE: a nested function captures a mutated collection
summary: A nested function refers to a MutableVector or MutableArray binding that its enclosing function mutates in place; it would observe a binding that changes.
index: errors
alias: closure captures a mutated vector; capture of a mutable place
keywords: MutableVector, MutableArray, closure, nested function, capture, place, value semantics
related: concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array concept.closures error.mutable-place-receiver error.affine-capture-unsupported
docs: MUTABLE-VECTOR.md MUTABLE-ARRAY.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE

== MEANING
A binding that a function mutates in place (the root of a mutation's
receiver: `log.push(2)`) is a place. Bindings in Botlish never change,
and a closure captures values; a nested function that refers to a place
would observe a binding that does change under it. Since a collection is
a value and not a shared reference, this is rejected rather than turned
into aliasing.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a nested helper that reads (or reports the length of) a vector the
    enclosing function keeps pushing to.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass the collection as an argument: the callee gets its own logical copy
(copy-on-write, so passing is cheap). A nested function that must change
it takes it as a parameter and returns the updated collection.

@example reject
import mutable_vector

fn fill() -> int:
    log = mutable_vector::from_list([1])
    log.push(2)
    fn size() -> int:
        log.length()
    size()

fill()
@caption `size` captures `log`, which `fill` mutates
@error CORE SEMANTIC MUTABLE-PLACE-CAPTURE
@at 7:9
@end

@example accept
import mutable_vector

fn fill() -> int:
    log = mutable_vector::from_list([1])
    log.push(2)
    fn size(v: MutableVector[int]) -> int:
        v.length()
    size(log)

fill()
@caption Passed as an argument, the nested function gets its own copy
@value 2
@end

@topic error.coroutine-binding-field
title: COROUTINE-BINDING-FIELD: a malformed coroutine binding
summary: A coroutine binding names its results in braces, `coroutine {step, first} = f(...)`; any other field, a nested pattern, a positional form or `coroutine NAME = ...` is a syntax error.
index: errors
alias: coroutine binding syntax; coroutine step first fields
keywords: coroutine, binding, step, first, braces, pattern, construction, syntax
related: concept.coroutines error.surface-syntax error.duplicate-field error.coroutine-rhs-not-yielding
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: COROUTINE-BINDING-FIELD
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | COROUTINE-BINDING-FIELD: *

== MEANING
A coroutine is started by a coroutine binding:

    coroutine {step, first} = worker(10)

which runs `worker(10)` to its first outward `yield` (or its return) and
binds two results: `step`, the handle that resumes it, and `first`, the
first outward value. Each field may be renamed (`{step: next, first:
initial}`) or omitted; no other field exists. The parser reports
COROUTINE-BINDING-FIELD (a syntax error whose message starts with the
code) for:

  * an unknown field (`coroutine {handle} = ...`);
  * a nested pattern in a field (`{step: {a}}`): destructure `first` in
    a separate statement;
  * the positional form (`coroutine (a, b) = ...`);
  * `coroutine step = worker(10)`: there is no sugar without braces.

A field written twice is DUPLICATE-FIELD. Outside this form, `coroutine`
is an ordinary name (`coroutine::done?`, a variable called coroutine).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `coroutine step = f()` written by analogy with an ordinary binding;
  * a misspelled field (`{steps}`, `{handle}`) or a pattern that
    destructures the first value in place.

== WHAT TO DO
Write the braced form with the fields you need: `coroutine {step} =
f(...)` keeps only the handle, `coroutine {first} = f(...)` only the
first value (the coroutine still runs eagerly and is then released).

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine step = counter()
step()
@caption The results of a coroutine binding are named in braces
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 7:11
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine {step, first} = counter()
[first, step()]
@caption `step` is the handle, `first` the first yielded value
@value [1, 3]
@end

@topic error.coroutine-contract
title: COROUTINE-CONTRACT: an invalid coroutine type or protocol
summary: A Coroutine{...} type resumes with no message or one struct message, and a coroutine cannot yield, return, receive or fail with affine values.
index: errors
alias: Coroutine type args; affine coroutine result; coroutine message must be a struct
keywords: Coroutine type, args, message, struct, affine result, yield, error payload, protocol
related: concept.coroutines concept.function-types concept.affine error.coroutine-resume-type error.coroutine-not-function
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-CONTRACT

== MEANING
A coroutine handle's type is written like a function type,
`Coroutine{args: [M], return: R, errors: [E]}`: `args` is its resume
protocol, `return` its outward type (what every yield and the final
return send), `errors` the declared errors a resume may raise.
COROUTINE-CONTRACT is reported for:

  * a `Coroutine{...}` type whose `args` lists more than one type, or one
    that is not a declared struct: a coroutine is resumed with no message
    or with exactly one structured message;
  * a construction whose coroutine would end its segments with an affine
    value (yield or return a handle, or a struct owning one): a completed
    coroutine returns its cached final result again on every later
    resume, which would give one affine value several owners;
  * a construction whose resume message type is affine: a message sent
    to a coroutine that has already completed is never received, so
    nothing would own or release it;
  * a construction whose coroutine may fail with an error whose payload
    is affine: a failed coroutine raises its cached error again on every
    later resume.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `Coroutine{args: [int], ...}` or two message types in `args`;
  * a coroutine that spawns workers and yields their handles;
  * a coroutine whose message or result struct has a handle field.

== WHAT TO DO
Put several message values in the fields of one struct and name that
struct in `args` (or the function's `resume` clause). Keep a coroutine's
outward values, messages and error payloads unrestricted; give it affine
values when it is constructed, as arguments of the coroutine's function
(a coroutine construction moves its arguments in).

@example reject
import coroutine

fn drive(s: Coroutine{args: [int], return: int}) -> int:
    s(1)

0
@caption A coroutine message is a struct, never an int
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-CONTRACT
@at 3:9
@end

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn make() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    coroutine {step} = counter()
    step

fn spawner() -> Coroutine{args: [], return: int}:
    yield make()
    make()

coroutine {step} = spawner()
0
@caption A coroutine may not yield or return a coroutine handle
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-CONTRACT
@at 15:1
@end

@topic error.coroutine-not-function
title: COROUTINE-NOT-FUNCTION: a coroutine handle passed as a function value
summary: A coroutine handle is passed where a structural Fn{...} type is declared; Coroutine and Fn are distinct callable kinds even with the same contract, with no conversion.
index: errors
alias: coroutine passed as a function value; Coroutine versus Fn
keywords: coroutine, Fn, function type, callable kind, contract, parameter, handle
related: concept.function-types concept.coroutines concept.affine error.coroutine-contract error.unwrapped-yield
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md STRUCTURAL-FUNCTION-TYPES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-NOT-FUNCTION

== MEANING
`Fn{args, return, errors}` and `Coroutine{args, return, errors}` share
one callable contract, but they are different kinds of callable: calling
a function value runs the same code each time, while calling a handle
resumes one evolving execution, which has one owner. A parameter
declared with an `Fn{...}` type admits exact functions and function
values only; passing a handle to it is COROUTINE-NOT-FUNCTION, even when
the contracts agree. (A handle given to an `Fn`-typed struct field or
function result is a TYPE error with the same explanation.) There is no
implicit conversion in either direction: a function where a Coroutine is
expected is a TYPE error too.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a higher-order helper declared `f: Fn{...}` reused for a coroutine.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the parameter with the coroutine type the message suggests
(`Coroutine{args: [], return: int}`), or leave it untyped: an untyped
parameter is specialized for the handle (the call becomes a resume) and
for functions alike.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn apply(f: Fn{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
    f()

coroutine {step} = counter()
apply(step)
@caption A handle is not a function value of the same contract
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-NOT-FUNCTION
@at 11:7
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

fn drive(s: Coroutine{args: [], return: int}) -> int:
    s()

coroutine {step} = counter()
drive(step)
@caption A parameter of the coroutine type takes the handle (and owns it)
@value 3
@end

@topic error.coroutine-result-mismatch
title: COROUTINE-RESULT-MISMATCH: yields and return have no common type
summary: A coroutine's yields and its final return share one outward type: its root function's declared result, or one type all the others fit.
index: errors
alias: coroutine outward type; yield type does not match the result
keywords: coroutine, yield, return, outward type, declared result, segment, root function
related: concept.coroutines concept.types error.coroutine-resume-conflict
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESULT-MISMATCH

== MEANING
Every call that starts or resumes a coroutine returns the value its
segment ends with: a `yield` sends a value outward, and the final
`return` (or last statement) of the coroutine's root function is the
last one. All of them have ONE type, the coroutine's outward type: the
root function's declared result type, else the one type among its return
and every yield it can reach (deep ones included) that all others are
subtypes of. When there is none, the construction is rejected. Against a
declared result the message names the offending yield; otherwise it
lists the types a segment can end with.

A helper function's own `return` goes to its caller as for any call; only
the root's return is outward.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * yielding progress values of one type and returning a final result of
    another type;
  * a deep helper that yields a value of another type than the root
    returns.

== WHAT TO DO
Use one type for both: a struct with the fields of the progress values
and of the final result (e.g. a `finished` flag), declared as the root's
result type.

@example reject
import coroutine

struct Progress:
    done: int

struct Report:
    total: int

fn work() -> Report:
    yield Progress {done: 1}
    Report {total: 2}

coroutine {step} = work()
0
@caption The yield sends a Progress, the declared result is Report
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESULT-MISMATCH
@at 13:1
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

struct Status:
    done: int
    finished: bool

fn work() -> Status:
    yield Status {done: 1, finished: false}
    Status {done: 2, finished: true}

coroutine {step, first} = work()
last = step()
[first.done, last.done]
@caption One outward type for every segment
@value [1, 2]
@end

@topic error.coroutine-resume-arity
title: COROUTINE-RESUME-ARITY: a resume with the wrong number of messages
summary: A coroutine with the zero-message protocol is resumed as step(), one with a struct protocol with exactly one message, step(Message {...}).
index: errors
alias: resume with an argument; wrong number of resume messages
keywords: coroutine, resume, message, protocol, zero-message, argument count, step
related: concept.coroutines error.coroutine-resume-type error.coroutine-resume-conflict error.arity
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-ARITY

== MEANING
Each coroutine has one fixed resume protocol, its root function's: either
no message (the default, when nothing determines a message type), or one
message of a declared struct type (declared by `resume Message`, or
inferred from how the yields' values are used). A resume passes
exactly that: `step()` for the zero-message protocol, `step(Message
{...})` for a struct protocol. Any other number of arguments is
COROUTINE-RESUME-ARITY; a message of another type is a TYPE error.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * passing a value to a coroutine that declares no `resume` clause;
  * passing several values instead of one struct.

== WHAT TO DO
Resume with the protocol the message states. To send values, declare
`resume T` (a struct with one field per value) as the last entry of the
root function's parameter list and read the fields of the yield's value.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine {step} = counter()
step(5)
@caption `counter` takes no message
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-ARITY
@at 8:1
@end

@topic error.coroutine-resume-conflict
title: COROUTINE-RESUME-CONFLICT: two resume protocols in one coroutine
summary: One coroutine has exactly one resume protocol; a function reaching yields with two different message types (declared or inferred) is rejected, with both chains.
index: errors
alias: two resume protocols; conflicting resume message types
keywords: coroutine, resume, protocol, message type, inference, yield, helper, conflict
related: concept.coroutines error.coroutine-resume-type error.coroutine-resume-underconstrained error.coroutine-resume-arity
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-CONFLICT

== MEANING
A yield evaluates to the message of the resume that continues the
coroutine, so every yield one coroutine can reach -- in its root function
and in every helper it calls, at any depth -- receives the same message
type. The compiler derives each yielding function's protocol from its
`resume T` clause, from how its yields' values are used (passed to a
parameter declared with a struct type), and from the yielding functions
it calls. Two different protocols meeting in one function are a conflict:
the message explains both through their call chains. No union of
message types is ever inferred.

A helper whose yields all discard their values adapts to its caller's
protocol. A helper whose yield value is used but nothing types it
defaults to the zero-message protocol, which conflicts with a struct
protocol of its caller.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * one coroutine calling two helpers written for different coroutines;
  * a helper that uses its yield's value without declaring `resume T`,
    called from a coroutine that declares one.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
Follow the two chains of the message ("resume Start: it calls first_half
(...), which declares it") to the declarations or uses that fix each
protocol. Protocols are inferred over every call written, reachable or
not, so a call in dead code still counts.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare one struct with the fields both need and use it as the protocol
everywhere, or split the work into two coroutines.

@example reject
import coroutine

struct Start:
    n: int

struct Stop:
    reason: str

struct Ev:
    n: int

fn first_half(resume Start) -> int:
    m = yield Ev {n: 1}
    m.n

fn second_half(resume Stop) -> int:
    m = yield Ev {n: 2}
    3

fn both() -> Ev:
    Ev {n: first_half() + second_half()}

coroutine {step} = both()
0
@caption `both` reaches yields of two different message types
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-CONFLICT
@at 20:8
@end

@topic error.coroutine-resume-type
title: COROUTINE-RESUME-TYPE: a resume clause that does not name a struct
summary: The type of a `resume T` clause must be a declared struct type; a coroutine is resumed with one structured message, never an int, String, List or anonymous struct.
index: errors
alias: resume clause type; resume message of type int
keywords: coroutine, resume clause, message, struct, protocol, parameter list
related: concept.coroutines concept.structs error.coroutine-contract error.coroutine-resume-arity
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-TYPE

== MEANING
`fn worker(seed: int, resume Message) -> Event` declares the message the
coroutine receives at each resume: every `yield` in it evaluates to a
Message. The protocol is one named struct type or nothing; scalars,
Strings, Lists and anonymous structs are rejected. Several values travel
as the fields of one struct, so a later protocol can grow by adding
fields.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * `resume int` or `resume str` for a coroutine fed single values;
  * `resume List[T]` for a batch of values.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare a struct with one field per value and name it in the clause:

    struct Input:
        n: int

    fn echo(resume Input) -> int:
        m = yield 0
        m.n

@example reject
import coroutine

fn echo(resume int) -> int:
    n = yield 0
    n

0
@caption The message type of a resume clause must be a struct
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-TYPE
@at 3:16
@end

@topic error.coroutine-resume-underconstrained
title: COROUTINE-RESUME-UNDERCONSTRAINED: the message type is not determined
summary: A yield's value has a field read, but no resume clause or typed use determines the message type of the function; declare `resume T`.
index: errors
alias: resume type not determined; field of a yield value
keywords: coroutine, yield, resume clause, message type, field, inference, protocol
related: concept.coroutines error.coroutine-resume-type error.coroutine-resume-conflict
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-UNDERCONSTRAINED

== MEANING
The message type of a yielding function is declared (`resume T`) or
inferred from how its yields' values are used: passed to a parameter of
an exact function declared with a struct type, or through the yielding
functions it calls. A field read (`m.n`) does not say which struct `m`
is, so a function whose yield value is projected needs its protocol from
somewhere else; with nothing to determine it, the yield is rejected,
naming the projection.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a function that reads fields of its yield's value but has no
    `resume` clause, while nothing else fixes the message type.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the protocol with `resume T` as the last entry of the function's
parameter list (after its parameters, flags and context parameters).

@example reject
import coroutine

struct Ev:
    n: int

fn echo() -> Ev:
    m = yield Ev {n: 0}
    Ev {n: m.n}

coroutine {step} = echo()
0
@caption Nothing says which struct the yield's value `m` is
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RESUME-UNDERCONSTRAINED
@at 7:9
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

struct In:
    n: int

struct Ev:
    n: int

fn echo(resume In) -> Ev:
    m = yield Ev {n: 0}
    Ev {n: m.n}

coroutine {step, first} = echo()
last = step(In {n: 7})
[first.n, last.n]
@caption The resume clause fixes the message type
@value [0, 7]
@end

@topic error.coroutine-rhs-not-yielding
title: COROUTINE-RHS-NOT-YIELDING: starting a coroutine of a non-yielding call
summary: The right side of a coroutine binding must be a direct call of a named function that may yield; a function that never yields, a function value or another expression is rejected.
index: errors
alias: coroutine of a function that never yields; start a coroutine of a plain function
keywords: coroutine, binding, construction, yield, direct call, function value
related: concept.coroutines error.coroutine-binding-field error.unwrapped-yield
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RHS-NOT-YIELDING

== MEANING
`coroutine {step, first} = f(args)` starts a coroutine of `f`. The
compiler tracks which functions may suspend (a reachable `yield` of their
own, or a reachable call of a function that may), so the right side must
be a direct call of a named function in that set. Two cases are
rejected:

  * the callee never yields: there is no coroutine to start, call it
    directly;
  * the right side is not a direct call of a named function (a function
    value, a parameter, any other expression): the compiler cannot see
    what it would run.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a coroutine binding of a function whose only yield is in a branch
    the compiler proves unreachable, or that has no yield at all;
  * `coroutine {step} = f` with `f` a function value or a parameter.

== WHAT TO DO
Call a function that never yields directly. If it should yield, check
that its `yield` is reachable (a yield in code proven unreachable does
not count). Start coroutines of named functions only; to choose among
several, wrap each construction in a function that returns the handle
and choose between the calls (`s = if c: make_a() else: make_b()`, with
one Coroutine{...} result type).

@example reject
import coroutine

fn plain() -> int:
    3

coroutine {step} = plain()
0
@caption `plain` never yields
@error CORE SEMANTIC COROUTINE-RHS-NOT-YIELDING
@at 6:1
@end

@topic error.unwrapped-yield
title: UNWRAPPED-YIELD: a suspending function called outside a coroutine
summary: A function that may yield is called from top-level code, or used as a function value, without a coroutine binding to start it.
index: errors
alias: call a yielding function directly; may suspend but does not start a coroutine
keywords: coroutine, yield, suspend, call chain, top level, function value, construction
related: concept.coroutines error.yield-outside-function error.coroutine-rhs-not-yielding error.coroutine-not-function
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC UNWRAPPED-YIELD

== MEANING
`yield` suspends the coroutine the code runs in, at any call depth, so a
function that may yield (directly, or through a function it calls) may
only run inside a coroutine. A reachable call of such a function from
top-level code does not start one: it is rejected, with the shortest
call chain to the yield. A call from another function that may yield is
fine -- that function runs inside a coroutine, or is itself reported
where it is called.

A function that may yield also cannot become a function value: a call
through an unknown function value could run it outside every coroutine.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * calling a generator-style function directly, expecting a value;
  * passing a yielding function to a higher-order function.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message prints the shortest call chain from the called function to
a yield ("..., which yields directly at FILE:LINE:COL").

== WHAT TO DO
Start it with a coroutine binding, `coroutine {step, first} = f(...)`,
and resume `step()` for the next values. Call it directly only from
functions that themselves run inside a coroutine.

@example reject
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

counter()
@caption A direct call does not start a coroutine
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNWRAPPED-YIELD
@at 7:1
@end

@example accept
import coroutine

fn counter() -> int:
    yield 1
    3

coroutine {step, first} = counter()
[first, step(), step()]
@caption Started by a binding; once completed, it repeats its result
@value [1, 3, 3]
@end

@topic error.yield-outside-function
title: YIELD-OUTSIDE-FUNCTION: a yield in top-level code
summary: `yield` suspends the coroutine whose function it is written in; top-level code is not in a function, so a yield there is rejected.
index: errors
alias: yield at the top level; top-level yield
keywords: coroutine, yield, top level, program, function
related: concept.coroutines error.unwrapped-yield error.yield-outside-coroutine
docs: COROUTINES.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC YIELD-OUTSIDE-FUNCTION

== MEANING
A coroutine is an execution of a function started by a coroutine
binding; a `yield` suspends that execution. The program's top-level code
is not a function and can never run as a coroutine, so a yield written
there has nothing to suspend.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * script-style code that yields progress values from the top level.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the yielding code into a function and start it with
`coroutine {step, first} = f(...)`.

@example reject
import coroutine

x = yield 3
x
@caption A yield in top-level code
@error CORE SEMANTIC YIELD-OUTSIDE-FUNCTION
@at 3:5
@end

@topic error.trait-nested
title: TRAIT-NESTED: a trait declared inside a function
summary: A trait (or context trait) declaration is allowed only at the top level of a module or the entry program, never inside a function, if or loop.
index: errors
alias: trait declared inside a function; nested trait declaration
keywords: trait, declaration, top level, nested, function
related: concept.traits concept.context-traits error.surface-syntax error.trait-nested-polymorphic
docs: TRAITS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: TRAIT-NESTED
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | TRAIT-NESTED: *

== MEANING
A trait is a named member of its module, like a struct: its identity is
`module::Name` (or the bare name in the entry program), other modules
import it with `import type`, and the compiler resolves every use of it
statically. A declaration nested in a function body would have no such
identity, so the parser rejects it (a syntax error whose message starts
with TRAIT-NESTED).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a trait written next to the only function that uses it, inside it.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the declaration to the top level of the file. Functions that use
the trait may stay where they are, but a function with a trait-typed
parameter must itself be top-level (error.trait-nested-polymorphic).

@example reject
fn f() -> int:
    trait Named:
        fn name(value: Named) -> str
    1

f()
@caption A trait declaration inside a function body
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:5
@end

@topic error.trait-empty
title: TRAIT-EMPTY: a trait with no requirement
summary: A trait (or context trait) declares at least one requirement in an indented block; a requirement-free trait would be satisfied by every type.
index: errors
alias: trait without requirements; marker trait
keywords: trait, requirement, declaration, marker, empty
related: concept.traits concept.context-traits error.surface-syntax
docs: TRAITS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: TRAIT-EMPTY
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | TRAIT-EMPTY: *

== MEANING
A type satisfies a trait structurally, by providing every requirement
in its owner namespace. With no requirement every type would satisfy it,
so a trait view of it would allow no operation and say nothing: marker
traits are not supported. The parser reports TRAIT-EMPTY (a syntax error
whose message starts with the code) when no indented block of
requirements follows `trait NAME:`.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a trait meant as a tag ("this type is serializable");
  * requirements not indented under `trait NAME:`.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the operations the trait's users need, each as a signature
whose first parameter is the trait itself (`fn name(value: Named) ->
str`); a context trait's requirements take ordinary parameters only. To
mark values, use a struct field or an enum instead.

@example reject
trait Marker:

0
@caption `Marker` declares no requirement
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:7
@end

@topic error.trait-requirement-body
title: TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-BODY: a trait requirement with a body
summary: A trait requirement is a signature only, with no body and no trailing colon; implementations are ordinary functions of the type's owner namespace.
index: errors
alias: trait requirement with a body; default method
keywords: trait, requirement, body, signature, default method, implementation
related: concept.traits error.surface-syntax error.trait-not-satisfied
docs: TRAITS.md
code: TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-BODY
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-BODY: *

== MEANING
A requirement such as `fn name(value: Named) -> str` states what a type
must provide; it has no code of its own. There are no default methods:
every satisfying type implements every requirement with an ordinary
top-level function of the same name in the namespace that owns the type.
A `:` after a requirement (the start of a body) is a syntax error whose
message starts with TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-BODY.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a default implementation written in the trait, as in languages that
    have default methods.

== WHAT TO DO
Remove the `:` and the body. Write the body as the implementation, next
to the concrete type: `fn name(value: Person) -> str:`. Shared code can
be an ordinary function taking a trait parameter (`fn describe(value:
Named) -> str`).

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str:
        "anonymous"

0
@caption A requirement has no body
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:33
@end

@topic error.trait-requirement-flags
title: TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-FLAGS: a trait requirement with flags
summary: A trait requirement declares no flag section; flags in trait requirements and implementations are not supported yet.
index: errors
alias: trait requirement with flags
keywords: trait, requirement, flags, flag section, signature
related: concept.traits concept.flags error.surface-syntax
docs: TRAITS.md FLAGS.md
code: TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-FLAGS
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-FLAGS: *

== MEANING
A requirement's signature has ordinary parameters (and a result and an
`errors` clause) only. Flag parameters (`flags :upper`) are not supported
in requirements -- and an implementation with flags does not satisfy a
requirement either. This is a limit of the current trait design; the
parser reports it as a syntax error whose message starts with
TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-FLAGS.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * an option of the operation written as a flag, as for an ordinary
    function.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass the option as an ordinary `bool` parameter of the requirement, or
declare two requirements (`name`, `upper_name`).

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named, flags :upper) -> str

0
@caption Flags are not supported in a requirement
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 2:33
@end

@topic error.trait-context-requirement
title: TRAIT-CONTEXT-REQUIREMENT: a trait requirement with a context parameter
summary: A requirement declares no context parameter: an ordinary trait's operations are not context-supplied, and a context trait's installed context is the implicit receiver.
index: errors
alias: trait requirement with a context parameter
keywords: trait, context trait, requirement, context parameter, receiver
related: concept.traits concept.context-traits concept.contexts error.surface-syntax
docs: TRAITS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: TRAIT-CONTEXT-REQUIREMENT
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | TRAIT-CONTEXT-REQUIREMENT: *

== MEANING
The parser rejects a context section (`context log: Log`) in a
requirement, with one of two messages:

  * in an ordinary trait: context-supplied trait operations are not
    supported yet; a requirement is an ordinary signature over the
    trait's own values;
  * in a context trait: the installed context is the requirement's
    implicit receiver, so a requirement declares only its ordinary
    parameters. The implementation, in the context's owner namespace,
    is the one that takes exactly one context parameter of its context
    type.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a requirement copied from an implementation that takes a context;
  * a context trait's requirement written with the context as an
    explicit parameter.

== WHAT TO DO
Drop the context parameter from the requirement. An implementation
cannot take one either, so pass what an operation needs from the
environment as an ordinary argument (its caller can read it through a
context parameter of its own). For an abstraction of the environment
itself, declare a `context trait`.

@example reject
context struct Log:
    prefix: str

trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named, context log: Log) -> str

0
@caption An ordinary trait's requirement takes no context parameter
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 5:35
@end

@topic error.trait-duplicate-requirement
title: TRAIT-DUPLICATE-REQUIREMENT: two requirements of one name
summary: A trait declares at most one requirement per operation name; there is no overloading.
index: errors
alias: trait requirement declared twice; overloaded trait requirement
keywords: trait, requirement, duplicate, overloading, operation name
related: concept.traits error.duplicate
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-DUPLICATE-REQUIREMENT

== MEANING
A trait operation is called by name (`value.name()`) and implemented by
the one function of that name in the type's owner namespace, which has
at most one binding per name. Two requirements of one name could never
both be implemented, and there are no overload sets to choose between
them, so the declaration is rejected.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * two arities of one operation, as overloads in other languages.

== WHAT TO DO
Give each operation its own name (`name`, `short_name`), or merge them
into one requirement with an extra ordinary parameter.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str
    fn name(value: Named, short: bool) -> str

0
@caption `name` is declared twice
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-DUPLICATE-REQUIREMENT
@at 3:8
@end

@topic error.trait-name-collision
title: TRAIT-NAME-COLLISION: a trait named like another type
summary: Traits share the type namespace: a trait cannot have the name of a built-in type, a struct, a type, a refinement or another trait of the same module.
index: errors
alias: trait name already declared; trait named like a struct
keywords: trait, name, type namespace, struct, built-in type, collision, declaration
related: concept.traits concept.types error.duplicate
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NAME-COLLISION

== MEANING
A trait's name is used where type names are (`value: Named`, `-> Named`,
`import type m::Named`), so it shares one namespace with the structs,
types (integer domains, refinements), enums and other traits of its
module, and it cannot reuse a built-in type name (`str`, `int`, `List`,
`Fn`, ...). A second declaration of the same identity is rejected at the
trait; an enum named like a trait is reported as TYPE, at the enum.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a trait named after the struct that will implement it;
  * a trait named like a built-in type.

== WHAT TO DO
Rename the trait (an adjective or capability name works well: `Named`,
`Measured`) or the colliding type.

@example reject
struct Named:
    value: str

trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

0
@caption A struct already has the name `Named`
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NAME-COLLISION
@at 4:7
@end

@topic error.trait-requirement-receiver
title: TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-RECEIVER: a requirement without the trait as receiver
summary: Every requirement of an ordinary trait has a first parameter declared as the trait itself, the receiver; static and constructor requirements are not supported.
index: errors
alias: trait requirement without a receiver; static trait requirement
keywords: trait, requirement, receiver, first parameter, static, constructor
related: concept.traits error.trait-self-position concept.method-sugar
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-RECEIVER

== MEANING
A trait operation is called on a value: `value.name()` inside a function
that takes `value: Named`. So every requirement's first ordinary
parameter must be declared with the trait's own type (`fn name(value:
Named) -> str`); it stands for the implementing type. A requirement
with no parameter, an untyped first parameter or a first parameter of
another type is rejected: there are no static or constructor
requirements. (A context trait's requirements are different: they have
no receiver parameter at all.)

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a constructor-like requirement (`fn parse(text: str) -> Parse`);
  * an untyped first parameter (`fn name(value) -> str`).

== WHAT TO DO
Make the first parameter the trait itself. To construct values, write an
ordinary function in the type's module, or a requirement that returns
the trait from an existing value (`fn reset(value: Parser) -> Parser`).

@example reject
trait Parse:
    fn parse(text: str) -> Parse

0
@caption A requirement must take the trait itself first
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-REQUIREMENT-RECEIVER
@at 2:20
@end

@topic error.trait-self-position
title: TRAIT-SELF-POSITION: the trait's own type in a later parameter
summary: A requirement may use the trait's own type only as its first parameter and as its result; relational requirements over two values of one witness are not supported yet.
index: errors
alias: relational trait requirement; trait type in a second parameter
keywords: trait, requirement, self type, relational, parameter, witness, equality
related: concept.traits error.trait-requirement-receiver
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-SELF-POSITION

== MEANING
Inside a requirement the trait's name means "the implementing type".
As the first parameter it is the receiver; as the result it says the
operation returns a value of the same type (`fn normalize(value: Norm)
-> Norm`). In any other parameter it would relate two values that must
have the same concrete type (`fn same?(a: Same, b: Same) -> bool`) -- a
relational requirement, which the current trait design does not support.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * an equality or ordering requirement over two values of the trait.

== WHAT TO DO
Compare through an unrestricted projection instead: a requirement that
returns a key (`fn key(value: Keyed) -> str`), compared by the caller. A
later parameter may have any ordinary type, or be untyped.

@example reject
trait Same:
    fn same?(a: Same, b: Same) -> bool

0
@caption The trait's own type in the second parameter
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-SELF-POSITION
@at 2:26
@end

@topic error.trait-other-trait
title: TRAIT-OTHER-TRAIT: a requirement that mentions another trait
summary: A requirement's parameters and result cannot mention another trait (or a context trait): trait composition and trait-typed results of other traits are not supported yet.
index: errors
alias: trait composition; requirement returning another trait
keywords: trait, context trait, requirement, composition, supertrait, parameter, result
related: concept.traits concept.context-traits error.trait-self-position
docs: TRAITS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-OTHER-TRAIT

== MEANING
Each requirement is checked against one witness, the implementing type.
A parameter or result typed with another trait (or a type containing
one, such as a List of it) would need a second witness chosen jointly
with the first; there is no trait composition, no supertrait and no
associated type. For a context trait, a requirement takes and returns
ordinary values only.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a requirement that returns or takes another trait, to compose two
    abstractions.

== WHAT TO DO
Use concrete types in the requirement, or split the work: one
trait-polymorphic function per trait, each taking its own trait
parameter (`fn show(a: Named, b: Tagged)` may take two different traits).

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

trait Tagged:
    fn tag(value: Tagged) -> Named

0
@caption A requirement of `Tagged` returns another trait
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-OTHER-TRAIT
@at 5:30
@end

@topic error.trait-not-satisfied
title: TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED: a value whose type does not satisfy the trait
summary: A value passed to (or returned as) a trait type does not satisfy it: its owner namespace lacks a compatible function for some requirement, or the type has no owner at all.
index: errors
alias: type does not implement the trait; missing trait implementation
keywords: trait, satisfy, conformance, implementation, owner namespace, witness, requirement, signature
related: concept.traits concept.hashable concept.modules error.trait-unknown-operation error.trait-view-misuse error.type
docs: TRAITS.md HASHABLE-AND-KEYED-HASH.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED

== MEANING
A concrete type satisfies a trait structurally: the namespace that owns
the type -- a struct's declaring module (or the entry program), `str`
for String, `char` for UnicodeChar -- declares, for every requirement, a
top-level function of that name whose declared signature fits (the
trait replaced by the type; parameters contravariant, result covariant,
errors a subset). Only the owner counts: imports and functions at the
call site do not. The message shows the first requirement that fails,
what was required and what was found.

Types with no owner satisfy nothing: `int`, `bool`, `any`, a List or
other applied type, a function. A view of one trait is not accepted as
another trait.

The built-in trait Hashable (concept.hashable) is satisfied differently:
the language supplies its witnesses -- every type whose values all have
total equality and a hash, `int`, Lists and structs of such types
included -- and the message names the field or element that is not
Hashable (a function has no total equality). The argument of `hash` is
held to it too when its type shows it is not Hashable.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * no implementation, or one with a different parameter or result type;
  * an implementation for another type of the same name: one namespace
    has one function per name, so two types of the entry program cannot
    both implement `name` -- give the second type a module of its own;
  * a value typed `any` (from a List of mixed values, an untyped
    parameter): its concrete type is not recovered;
  * an implementation with flags, context parameters or more declared
    errors than the requirement allows.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message shows the requirement ("required:", with the trait replaced
by the argument's type), the function the owner namespace has under that
name ("found:", if any) and, last, why it does not fit.

== WHAT TO DO
Declare or fix the function in the type's owner module. Values reaching
the trait parameter must have a concrete static type.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Person:
    value: str

fn name(value: Person) -> str:
    value.value

fn describe(value: Named) -> str:
    value.name()

struct Robot:
    serial: int

describe(Robot {serial: 7})
@caption The entry program's `name` takes a Person, not a Robot
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NOT-SATISFIED
@at 16:10
@end

@example accept
trait Measured:
    fn length(value: Measured) -> int

fn size(value: Measured) -> int:
    value.length()

size("hello")
@caption String satisfies `Measured` through its owner's `str::length`
@value 5
@end

@topic error.trait-unknown-operation
title: TRAIT-UNKNOWN-OPERATION: an operation the trait does not declare
summary: On a trait view (or a context-trait binding) only the trait's requirements can be called; any other method call is rejected, whatever the concrete type provides.
index: errors
alias: method not declared by the trait; unknown trait operation
keywords: trait, context trait, view, operation, method call, requirement, duck typing
related: concept.traits concept.context-traits error.trait-view-misuse error.no-applicable-method
docs: TRAITS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-UNKNOWN-OPERATION

== MEANING
Inside a function that takes `value: Named`, `value` is a trait view:
source code may use exactly the trait's requirements, as method calls.
A generic body is checked once, against the trait alone -- also in a
function nobody calls -- so an operation the trait does not declare is
rejected even if every type passed in happens to provide it. The same
holds for a context-trait binding (`context clock: Clock`) and its
trait's operations. The message lists the operations that exist.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a function of the concrete type called on the view, expecting it to
    be found on the type underneath;
  * a misspelled operation name.

== WHAT TO DO
Add the operation to the trait as a requirement (every satisfying type
must then implement it), or take a concrete type instead of the trait.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

fn shout(value: Named) -> str:
    value.upper()

0
@caption `Named` declares only `name`
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-UNKNOWN-OPERATION
@at 5:11
@end

@topic error.trait-view-misuse
title: TRAIT-VIEW-MISUSE: a trait view used as its concrete value
summary: A trait view exposes only its trait's operations: no field access, no use as a condition, loop bound or callee, no type test and no kind-specific native.
index: errors
alias: field of a trait view; downcast a trait view
keywords: trait, view, field, type test, cast, downcast, condition, native, witness
related: concept.traits error.trait-unknown-operation error.trait-not-satisfied
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-VIEW-MISUSE

== MEANING
A trait is a one-way view: a concrete value enters it at a trait-typed
parameter or result, and from then on the source sees only the trait.
The compiler keeps the concrete type as hidden provenance to specialize
the code, but nothing recovers it: reading a field (`value.value`),
using the view as an `if` condition, a loop operand or a callee, testing
its kind (`string?`) or giving it to a native that needs a kind of value
(`+`) is rejected. A trait has no run-time membership test, cast or
downcast.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a field of the concrete type read through the trait parameter;
  * a trait parameter used directly as a String or number.

== WHAT TO DO
Express what the code needs as a requirement of the trait (`fn
name(value: Named) -> str`) and call it, or take the concrete type as
the parameter's type.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Person:
    value: str

fn name(value: Person) -> str:
    value.value

fn raw(value: Named) -> str:
    value.value

raw(Person {value: "Ada"})
@caption A view has no fields
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-VIEW-MISUSE
@at 11:11
@end

@topic error.trait-witness-join
title: TRAIT-WITNESS-JOIN: one expression may hold views of different types
summary: Two trait views with different concrete witnesses meet (an if, a handler, a function's exits); heterogeneous trait values are not supported, as no erased trait representation exists.
index: errors
alias: heterogeneous trait values; trait object
keywords: trait, witness, join, if, result, heterogeneous, trait object, existential
related: concept.traits error.trait-storage-unsupported error.trait-view-misuse
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-WITNESS-JOIN

== MEANING
Every trait-typed value has one concrete witness known at compile time,
which is how the compiler turns trait operations into direct calls (one
specialized copy per witness). When two views of one trait with
different witnesses meet -- the branches of an `if`, a handler's value,
the exits of a function declared `-> Named` -- the result would need a
run-time choice between witnesses. There is no trait object: such a join
is rejected, never erased. Inside a generic body two different trait
parameters have different (abstract) witnesses, even if every caller
passes one type.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a function declared `-> Named` that returns one of its two trait
    parameters, or different concrete types on different paths;
  * an `if` choosing between two trait views.

== WHAT TO DO
Apply the trait operation in each branch and join the unrestricted
results (an int, a str), or return a concrete type.

@example reject
trait Measured:
    fn length(value: Measured) -> int

struct Box:
    items: int

fn length(value: Box) -> int:
    value.items

fn larger(a: Measured, b: Measured) -> Measured:
    if a.length() >= b.length():
        a
    else:
        b

larger("abc", Box {items: 2})
@caption `a` and `b` may have different concrete types
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-WITNESS-JOIN
@at 11:5
@end

@example accept
trait Measured:
    fn length(value: Measured) -> int

struct Box:
    items: int

fn length(value: Box) -> int:
    value.items

fn longest(a: Measured, b: Measured) -> int:
    if a.length() >= b.length():
        a.length()
    else:
        b.length()

"abc".longest(Box {items: 5})
@caption The branches join two ints instead of two views
@value 5
@end

@topic error.trait-storage-unsupported
title: TRAIT-STORAGE-UNSUPPORTED: a trait as a field or element type
summary: A struct field, an error payload field or a container element (List, MutableArray, ImmutableSet, a result such as List[Named]) cannot have a trait type.
index: errors
alias: trait typed struct field; list of trait values
keywords: trait, storage, field, List, container, element, payload, heterogeneous collection
related: concept.traits error.trait-witness-join concept.structs concept.error-payloads
docs: TRAITS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-STORAGE-UNSUPPORTED

== MEANING
A stored value has one concrete representation. A trait-typed field or
element would have to store a witness beside the value (an existential
representation), which Botlish does not have, so `struct Card: owner:
Named`, `List[Named]`, `MutableArray[Named]`, `ImmutableSet[Named]` and
an error payload field of a trait type are rejected where the type is
written. Heterogeneous trait collections are not supported.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a struct or List meant to hold "anything that is Named";
  * a function returning `List[Named]`.

== WHAT TO DO
Store the concrete type (`owner: Person`), and use the trait at function
boundaries only (`fn describe(value: Named)`). For values of several
types, store what the code needs from each (e.g. the result of calling
the trait operation), or one field per type.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Card:
    owner: Named

0
@caption A struct field stores one concrete type
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-STORAGE-UNSUPPORTED
@at 5:12
@end

@topic error.trait-polymorphic-function-value
title: TRAIT-POLYMORPHIC-FUNCTION-VALUE: a trait function used as a value
summary: A function with a trait parameter or result can be called directly or through an immutable alias, never passed, stored or returned as a function value; Fn types cannot mention a trait.
index: errors
alias: trait function as a function value; Fn type over a trait
keywords: trait, function value, higher-order, alias, Fn type, witness, dispatch
related: concept.traits concept.function-types error.trait-nested-polymorphic
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-POLYMORPHIC-FUNCTION-VALUE

== MEANING
A function such as `fn describe(value: Named) -> str` is compiled once
per concrete witness its calls pass (`describe<Person>`). Each call
needs its witness at compile time; a function value -- an argument, a
List element, a returned function, the value of a body -- has no place
for it, and there is no run-time trait dispatch to supply it. So the
function may be called directly or through an immutable alias (`f =
describe; f(p)`), and nothing else. A structural function type
mentioning a trait (`Fn{args: [Named], ...}`) is rejected for the same
reason.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a trait-polymorphic function passed to a higher-order function, put
    in a List or returned;
  * an `Fn{args: [Named], ...}` parameter type.

== WHAT TO DO
Call the function directly. To pass behaviour around, wrap one concrete
use in an ordinary function (`fn describe_person(p: Person) -> str:
describe(p)`) and pass that.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Person:
    value: str

fn name(value: Person) -> str:
    value.value

fn describe(value: Named) -> str:
    value.name()

fn apply(f, x):
    f(x)

apply(describe, Person {value: "Ada"})
@caption `describe` cannot be passed as a value
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-POLYMORPHIC-FUNCTION-VALUE
@at 16:7
@end

@example accept
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Person:
    value: str

fn name(value: Person) -> str:
    value.value

fn describe(value: Named) -> str:
    value.name()

say = describe
say(Person {value: "Ada"})
@caption An immutable alias called directly is the specialized call
@value "Ada"
@end

@topic error.trait-nested-polymorphic
title: TRAIT-NESTED-POLYMORPHIC: a nested function with a trait parameter
summary: A function with a trait-typed parameter must be declared at the top level of a module or the entry program; nested trait-polymorphic functions are not supported yet.
index: errors
alias: nested trait polymorphic function; trait parameter in a nested function
keywords: trait, nested function, specialization, monomorphization, top level
related: concept.traits error.affine-generic-nested-unsupported error.trait-nested
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NESTED-POLYMORPHIC

== MEANING
Trait-polymorphic functions are monomorphized: the compiler makes one
copy per witness and places it at the top level, before the code that
first needs it. A function declared inside another function would need
its copies made per activation of the enclosing function (it may capture
its locals), which the current specialization does not do. This is a
limit of the implementation.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a helper with a trait parameter declared inside the function that
    uses it.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the function to the top level, passing what it captured as
parameters, or give the nested function a concrete parameter type. A
nested closure inside a top-level trait-polymorphic function may capture
a view: it is part of each specialization.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Person:
    value: str

fn name(value: Person) -> str:
    value.value

fn greet(p: Person) -> str:
    fn inner(v: Named) -> str:
        v.name()
    inner(p)

greet(Person {value: "Ada"})
@caption `inner` takes a trait parameter but is nested
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-NESTED-POLYMORPHIC
@at 11:13
@end

@topic error.trait-polymorphic-recursion
title: TRAIT-POLYMORPHIC-RECURSION: recursion that changes the trait witness
summary: Specializations for different witnesses that call each other in a cycle are rejected: each would need the other bound first.
index: errors
alias: polymorphic recursion; recursion across trait witnesses
keywords: trait, recursion, witness, specialization, clone, cycle, mutual recursion
related: concept.traits concept.mutual-recursion concept.definition-order error.trait-impl-order
docs: TRAITS.md MUTUAL-RECURSION.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-POLYMORPHIC-RECURSION

== MEANING
A recursive trait-polymorphic function is specialized once per witness,
each copy calling itself. If the recursion passes a value of another
concrete type (`flip(v: Measured, n)` calling `flip("ab", ...)` and
`flip(Box {...}, ...)`), the copies for the two types call each other;
in Botlish a function can only call functions bound before it, and two
specializations cannot both be first. The message names the cycle.
Swapping two parameters of the same witness is ordinary self recursion
and fine.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a recursive function that alternates between values of different
    concrete types through one trait parameter;
  * swapping two trait parameters of different witnesses in a recursive
    call.

== WHAT TO DO
Keep the witness unchanged across the recursion: recurse on the same
view and handle the other types in a separate, non-recursive call, or
convert to a concrete type (the trait operation's result) before
recursing.

@example reject
trait Measured:
    fn length(value: Measured) -> int

struct Box:
    items: int

fn length(value: Box) -> int:
    value.items

fn flip(v: Measured, n: int) -> int:
    if n == 0:
        v.length()
    else:
        if mod(n, 2) == 0:
            flip("ab", n - 1)
        else:
            flip(Box {items: 3}, n - 1)

flip("x", 3)
@caption flip<str> and flip<Box> would call each other
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-POLYMORPHIC-RECURSION
@at 10:8
@end

@topic error.trait-impl-order
title: TRAIT-IMPL-ORDER: an implementation declared after its first use
summary: A trait operation's implementation must be declared before the code that first needs a specialization calling it: the ordinary no-forward-reference rule.
index: errors
alias: trait implementation declared too late
keywords: trait, implementation, order, forward reference, specialization, definition order
related: concept.traits concept.definition-order rule.forward-references error.trait-polymorphic-recursion
docs: TRAITS.md STRICT-REFERENCE-DETERMINISM.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-IMPL-ORDER

== MEANING
Bindings are visible from their definition on, and functions cannot
call functions defined after the statement that runs them. A trait
operation becomes a direct call of its implementation in a
specialization placed just before the statement that first needs it;
when the implementation is defined after that statement, the
specialization would refer forward. Whether a type satisfies the trait
does not depend on order -- only where the implementation is defined
relative to its first use.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * implementations written at the end of the file, after the top-level
    code that uses them;
  * a type and its functions declared below a top-level binding that
    already calls a trait-polymorphic function with it.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message names the specialization (`describe<Person>`), the statement
it is needed before, and the implementation that comes too late.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the implementation (and the type) above the first statement that
calls the trait-polymorphic function with that type.

@example reject
trait Named:
    fn name(value: Named) -> str

struct Person:
    value: str

fn describe(value: Named) -> str:
    value.name()

greeting = describe(Person {value: "Ada"})

fn name(value: Person) -> str:
    value.value

greeting
@caption `name` is defined after the statement that needs describe<Person>
@error CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-IMPL-ORDER
@at 8:5
@end

@topic error.trait-instance-budget
title: TRAIT-INSTANCE-BUDGET: no specialization for a trait-polymorphic call
summary: A call of a trait-polymorphic function has no static specialization because the semantic-instance analysis declined it, and there is no run-time trait dispatch to fall back to.
index: errors
alias: trait specialization budget
keywords: trait, specialization, budget, semantic instance, nesting limit, monomorphization
related: concept.traits concept.specialization error.affine-instance-budget
docs: TRAITS.md STATIC-DECISION-ECONOMICS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-INSTANCE-BUDGET

== MEANING
Every call of a trait-polymorphic function must be specialized for its
witnesses: there is no generic, dynamically dispatched version to call
instead. Specializations are made by the semantic-instance analysis,
which has budgets; past the instance-count budgets a trait call still
gets an instance keyed by its witnesses alone, so only the analysis's
nesting limit can leave one without. This is a limit of the compiler,
not a language rule, and ordinary programs do not reach it: a chain of
150 trait-polymorphic functions calling each other still compiles. A
call that can never run (an argument that always fails) is not reported:
it gets no specialization and needs none.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * very deep nesting of calls between trait-polymorphic functions.

== WHAT TO DO
Reduce the nesting of trait-polymorphic calls (give inner helpers
concrete parameter types), and report the program if it is small.

@topic error.trait-internal
title: TRAIT-INTERNAL: an internal failure of trait specialization
summary: An invariant of the trait monomorphization plan failed; this is a compiler bug, never a problem of the program.
index: errors
alias: trait specialization bug
keywords: trait, internal, compiler bug, monomorphization, plan
related: concept.traits error.context-trait-internal
docs: TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TRAIT-INTERNAL

== MEANING
After a program passes every check, the compiler plans its trait
specializations (which copy each call uses, which implementation each
trait operation calls). TRAIT-INTERNAL means the plan found something
the checks should have excluded -- a missing witness, conflicting
specializations of one expression.

== WHAT TO DO
You should never see this: it is a compiler bug. Please report the
program (as small as you can make it) with the full message.

@topic error.ambiguous-context-implementation
title: AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION: two providers of one context trait
summary: A call needs a context trait (such as io::IO) and more than one installed context implements it; the compiler never ranks providers, so exactly one must be installed.
index: errors
alias: two contexts implement the same context trait; ambiguous io provider
keywords: context trait, installed context, provider, selection, with context, io::IO, LinuxIO, ranking
related: concept.context-traits concept.contexts rule.context-installation error.missing-context stdlib.module.io stdlib.module.linux::io
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md CONTEXTS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION

== MEANING
A function whose requirement includes a context trait (directly, as
`context io: IO`, or through the functions it calls) is compiled against
the one installed context that implements the trait: at every reachable
top-level call the compiler collects the contexts installed at that
point (`with context ...`, in source order) that satisfy the trait, and
selects one statically. With none it reports MISSING-CONTEXT; with
several, AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION, listing every provider and the
call chain to the requirement. There is no ranking, no "most specific"
rule and no preference for the first or last installation: the same
program text must mean one implementation.

A coroutine's providers are chosen where it is constructed; a new
construction after a second installation is ambiguous too.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * installing a test or capture context for io::IO beside
    `linux::io::create()`;
  * two libraries each installing a context that implements one trait.

== HOW TO INVESTIGATE
The message lists every installed provider of the trait, in installation
order, then the call chain from the rejected call to the function that
requires the trait.

== WHAT TO DO
Install exactly one context implementing the trait before the calls that
need it. Installation is per program, so a program that needs a
different provider (a test) installs only that one.

@example reject
import io
import linux::io

context struct Capture:
    lines: int

fn write_text(text: str, context c: Capture) -> unit:
    unit

fn write_error_text(text: str, context c: Capture) -> unit:
    unit

fn hello() -> unit errors io::WriteFailed:
    io::print_line("hello")

with context Capture {lines: 0}
with context linux::io::create()
hello()
@caption Capture and LinuxIO both implement io::IO
@error CORE SEMANTIC AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION
@at 18:1
@end

@example accept
import io

context struct Capture:
    lines: int

fn write_text(text: str, context c: Capture) -> unit:
    unit

fn write_error_text(text: str, context c: Capture) -> unit:
    unit

fn hello() -> unit errors io::WriteFailed:
    io::print_line("hello")

with context Capture {lines: 0}
hello():
    on io::WriteFailed:
        unit
@caption One provider installed: io::print_line writes through Capture
@value unit
@end

@topic error.context-trait-misuse
title: CONTEXT-TRAIT-MISUSE: a context-trait binding used as a value
summary: A context-trait binding (`context io: IO`) is not a value: its only use is calling one of the trait's operations on it; it cannot be passed, stored, returned, compared, projected or tested.
index: errors
alias: pass a context trait binding; context trait binding is not a value
keywords: context trait, context parameter, binding, value, operation, method call, io::IO
related: concept.context-traits concept.contexts error.trait-unknown-operation error.context-trait-position
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TRAIT-MISUSE

== MEANING
Inside `fn print_line(text: str, context io: IO)`, `io` stands for
whichever installed context implements IO; the compiler selects it
statically and turns `io.write_text(...)` into a direct call of that
context's implementation. The binding has no value, slot or run-time
representation, so every reference to it must be the receiver of a
method call of one of the trait's operations. Returning it, passing it
to a function, storing it, comparing it, reading a field of the hidden
context or testing it is rejected -- also in a function nobody calls.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * passing `io` on to a helper instead of letting the helper declare
    its own context parameter;
  * comparing, storing or returning the binding.

== WHAT TO DO
Call the operation where the binding is (`io.write_text(t)`). A function
that needs the capability declares its own context parameter of the
trait (`context io: IO`); the requirement propagates to its callers with
no argument passing.

@example reject
context trait Clock:
    fn now() -> int

fn clock_of(context clock: Clock):
    clock

0
@caption A context-trait binding cannot be returned
@error CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TRAIT-MISUSE
@at 5:5
@end

@example accept
context trait Clock:
    fn now() -> int

context struct Fixed:
    at: int

fn now(context c: Fixed) -> int:
    c.at

fn later(context clock: Clock) -> int:
    clock.now() + 1

with context Fixed {at: 41}
later()
@caption Calling the trait's operation is the binding's one use
@value 42
@end

@topic error.context-trait-position
title: CONTEXT-TRAIT-POSITION: a context trait used as a value type
summary: A context trait is valid only as the type of a context parameter; as an ordinary parameter, a result, a field, a payload field, an element or part of a function type it is rejected.
index: errors
alias: context trait as a parameter type; context trait as a field type
keywords: context trait, context parameter, value type, parameter, field, List element, function type
related: concept.context-traits concept.contexts error.context-trait-misuse error.not-a-context
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TRAIT-POSITION

== MEANING
A context trait (`context trait Clock:`) is an execution-environment
capability supplied by an installed context, not a type of values: it
has no value, representation or storage. It may only be the type of a
context parameter, `context clock: Clock`. Everywhere a value type goes
-- an ordinary parameter, a function's result type, a struct field, an
error payload field, a List, MutableVector or MutableArray element, an
argument or result of an `Fn{...}` type -- it is rejected where the type
is written.

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a context trait written as an ordinary parameter type, without the
    `context` keyword;
  * a struct field or List meant to keep the capability.

== WHAT TO DO
For a parameter, move it to the function's context section, as the
message suggests: `fn elapsed(context clock: Clock)`. Callers then pass
nothing; the program installs a context implementing the trait. To keep
a value obtained from the environment, store the operation's result
(`clock.now()`), not the capability.

@example reject
context trait Clock:
    fn now() -> int

fn elapsed(clock: Clock) -> int:
    0

0
@caption A context trait as the type of an ordinary parameter
@error CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TRAIT-POSITION
@at 4:12
@end

@topic error.context-trait-internal
title: CONTEXT-TRAIT-INTERNAL: inconsistent context-trait selection
summary: Two calls selected different installed contexts for one context trait, which installation order makes impossible; this is a compiler bug.
index: errors
alias: context trait selection bug
keywords: context trait, selection, installed context, internal, compiler bug
related: concept.context-traits error.ambiguous-context-implementation error.trait-internal
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TRAIT-INTERNAL

== MEANING
Contexts are only ever added, in source order, so every call that finds
exactly one installed provider of a context trait finds the same one.
The compiler records that one selection per trait for the whole program;
CONTEXT-TRAIT-INTERNAL means two calls disagreed.

== WHAT TO DO
You should never see this: it is a compiler bug. Please report the
program (as small as you can make it) with the full message.
